  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Ohio has received $5.2B in federal contract obligations so far in FY2026 to date. There were 44,789 awards, and the average award size was $116K. Obligations fell 47.2% year over year. By fiscal year, obligations were $8.4B in FY21, $7.8B in FY22, $10.1B in FY23, $9B in FY24, $9.8B in FY25, and $5.2B in FY26. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $3.5B, followed by the General Services Administration with $403M and the Department of Energy with $333M. The top vendors include GENERAL ELECTRIC COMPANY at $543M and MARATHON PETROLEUM COMPANY LP at $411M, with SOUTHERN OHIO CLEANUP COMPANY LLC at $243M. Major industry categories by obligation include Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $818M, Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing at $501M, and Petroleum Refineries at $457M. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 47.2% year over year. That drop shows in the FY26 to date total of $5.2B compared with prior full-year totals like $9.8B in FY25 and $10.1B in FY23. The fall is broad enough to reduce the overall award volume while keeping an average award size of $116K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where is the money concentrated?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense accounts for the largest share at $3.5B. Other concentrated buying is GSA at $403M and the Department of Energy at $333M. On the vendor side, GENERAL ELECTRIC COMPANY received $543M and MARATHON PETROLEUM COMPANY LP received $411M."
      }
    ]
  },
